76 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518643 1077 "field-1" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518643 1078 #edb/ref 17592186518652 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518643 1079 #uuid "2abdfe3f-de40-5a85-8841-f40f55968b8d" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518644 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518666 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518644 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518644 1071 "The least common multiple of the polynomials $15(x+7)^{3}(y+2)^{3}(x-3),\\,9(x+7)(y+2)^{4}(x-3),$ and $6(x+7)(y+2)^{2}(x-3)^{4}$ is {{field-1}}." 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518644 1072 #uuid "31418054-5124-54fa-aa12-0062e192742c" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518644 1074 "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of three polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(15,9,6):$\n\n- Multiples of $15:$ $\\,15,\\,30,\\,45,\\,60,\\,75,\\,90,\\,105,…$\n\n- Multiples of $9:$ $\\,9,\\,18,\\,…,\\,81,\\,90,\\,99,…$\n\n- Multiples of $6:$ $\\,6,\\,12,\\,…,\\,84,\\,90,\\,96,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(15,9,6)=90.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(x+7)^{3}(y+2)^{3}(x-3),(x+7)(y+2)^{4}(x-3),(x+7)(y+2)^{2}(x-3)^{4},$ we find the largest powers for $(x+7)$, $(y+2)$, $(x-3)$ that appear in all these expressions:\n\n- For $(x+7)^{3},$ $(x+7),$ and $(x+7),$ the largest power is $3.$\n\n- For $(y+2)^{3},$ $(y+2)^{4},$ and $(y+2)^{2},$ the largest power is $4.$\n\n- For $(x-3),$ $(x-3),$ and $(x-3)^{4},$ the largest power is $4.$\n\nNow, we raise $(x+7)$, $(y+2)$, $(x-3)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((x+7)^{3}(y+2)^{3}(x-3),\\,(x+7)(y+2)^{4}(x-3),\\,(x+7)(y+2)^{2}(x-3)^{4})=(x+7)^{3}\\cdot (y+2)^{4}\\cdot (x-3)^{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{LCM}(15(x+7)^{3}(y+2)^{3}(x-3),\\,9(x+7)(y+2)^{4}(x-3),\\,6(x+7)(y+2)^{2}(x-3)^{4}) & = \\\\ 90\\cdot (x+7)^{3}\\cdot (y+2)^{4}\\cdot (x-3)^{4} & = \\\\ 90(x+7)^{3}(y+2)^{4}(x-3)^{4} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518645 1075 #edb/ref 17592186518649 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518645 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518645 1077 "field-1" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518645 1078 #edb/ref 17592186518649 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518645 1079 #uuid "337190dc-3336-5a50-afe1-252fa4610d47" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518646 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518660 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518646 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518646 1071 "The least common multiple of the polynomials $20(x-4)^{2}(y-6)^{4},\\,15(x-4)^{3}(y-6)^{6},$ and $15(x-4)(y-6)^{2}$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518646 1072 #uuid "3847fc1e-bdf1-5597-8649-8953329fb3af" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518646 1074 "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of three polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(20,15,15):$\n\n- Multiples of $20:$ $\\,20,\\,40,\\,60,\\,80,…$\n\n- Multiples of $15:$ $\\,15,\\,30,\\,45,\\,60,\\,75,…$\n\n- Multiples of $15:$ $\\,15,\\,30,\\,45,\\,60,\\,75,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(20,15,15)=60.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(x-4)^{2}(y-6)^{4},(x-4)^{3}(y-6)^{6},$ and $(x-4)(y-6)^{2},$ we find the largest powers for $(x-4)$ and $(y-6)$ that appear in all these expressions:\n\n- For $(x-4)^{2},(x-4)^{3},$ and $(x-4),$ the largest power is $3.$\n\n- For $(y-6)^{4},(y-6)^{6},$ and $(y-6)^{2},$ the largest power is $6.$\n\nNow, we raise $(x-4)$ and $(y-6)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((x-4)^{2}(y-6)^{4},(x-4)^{3}(y-6)^{6},(x-4)(y-6)^{2})=(x-4)^{3}\\cdot (y-6)^{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{LCM}(20(x-4)^{2}(y-6)^{4},\\,15(x-4)^{3}(y-6)^{6},\\,15(x-4)(y-6)^{2}) & = \\\\ 60\\cdot (x-4)^{3}\\cdot (y-6)^{6} & = \\\\ 60(x-4)^{3}(y-6)^{6} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518647 1075 #edb/ref 17592186518640 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518647 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518647 1077 "field-1" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518647 1078 #edb/ref 17592186518640 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518647 1079 #uuid "46176ccc-417a-575e-8cb4-ae6ce60f3156" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518648 1075 #edb/ref 17592186518656 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518648 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518648 1077 "field-1" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518648 1078 #edb/ref 17592186518656 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518648 1079 #uuid "468473bb-e1b2-5221-8aa6-70934c0379a9" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518649 1080 "30(x+4)^{4}(y+3)^{3}(x-4)^{3}" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518649 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518649 1083 #uuid "47d70ae6-b613-5da4-85ba-388541939ddd" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518650 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518648 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518650 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518650 1071 "The least common multiple of the polynomials $4(x+5)^{4}(y+2)^{3}(x+4),\\,2(x+5)^{2}(y+2)^{4}(x+4)^{4},$ and $3(x+5)^{3}(y+2)(x+4)^{5}$ is {{field-1}}." 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518650 1072 #uuid "48cdd375-1d58-5b9a-b9df-d62ad004db96" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518650 1074 "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of three polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(4,2,3):$\n\n- Multiples of $4:$ $\\,4,\\,8,\\,12,\\,16,…$\n\n- Multiples of $2:$ $\\,2,\\,4,\\,6,\\,8,\\,10,\\,12,\\,14,…$\n\n- Multiples of $3:$ $\\,3,\\,6,\\,9,\\,12,\\,15,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(4,2,3)=12.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(x+5)^{4}(y+2)^{3}(x+4),(x+5)^{2}(y+2)^{4}(x+4)^{4},(x+5)^{3}(y+2)(x+4)^{5},$ we find the largest powers for $(x+5)$, $(y+2)$, $(x+4)$ that appear in all these expressions:\n\n- For $(x+5)^{4},$ $(x+5)^{2},$ and $(x+5)^{3},$ the largest power is $4.$\n\n- For $(y+2)^{3},$ $(y+2)^{4},$ and $(y+2),$ the largest power is $4.$\n\n- For $(x+4),$ $(x+4)^{4},$ and $(x+4)^{5},$ the largest power is $5.$\n\nNow, we raise $(x+5)$, $(y+2)$, $(x+4)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((x+5)^{4}(y+2)^{3}(x+4),\\,(x+5)^{2}(y+2)^{4}(x+4)^{4},\\,(x+5)^{3}(y+2)(x+4)^{5})=(x+5)^{4}\\cdot (y+2)^{4}\\cdot (x+4)^{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{LCM}(4(x+5)^{4}(y+2)^{3}(x+4),\\,2(x+5)^{2}(y+2)^{4}(x+4)^{4},\\,3(x+5)^{3}(y+2)(x+4)^{5}) & = \\\\ 12\\cdot (x+5)^{4}\\cdot (y+2)^{4}\\cdot (x+4)^{5} & = \\\\ 12(x+5)^{4}(y+2)^{4}(x+4)^{5} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518651 1080 "24(y-5)^{4}" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518651 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518651 1083 #uuid "509c3585-5de6-532d-b3cb-6d1d008538cb" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518652 1080 "30(x+2)^{5}(y+5)^{5}(x-6)^{5}" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518652 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518652 1083 #uuid "68983c4c-47b1-5c21-a2a4-682c3f4cd41e" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518653 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518647 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518653 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518653 1071 "The least common multiple of the polynomials $12(x+2)^{5}(y-7)^{7},\\,8(x+2)^{3}(y-7)^{6},$ and $9(x+2)^{6}(y-7)^{6}$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518653 1072 #uuid "692901e0-19e7-57fc-a6a6-a8f6ea3938a2" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518653 1074 "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of three polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(12,8,9):$\n\n- Multiples of $12:$ $\\,12,\\,24,\\,36,\\,48,\\,60,\\,72,\\,84,…$\n\n- Multiples of $8:$ $\\,8,\\,16,\\,24,\\,32,\\,40,\\,48,\\,56,\\,64,\\,72,\\,80,…$\n\n- Multiples of $9:$ $\\,9,\\,18,\\,⋯,\\,63,\\,72,\\,81,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(12,8,9)=72.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(x+2)^{5}(y-7)^{7},(x+2)^{3}(y-7)^{6},$ and $(x+2)^{6}(y-7)^{6},$ we find the largest powers for $(x+2)$ and $(y-7)$ that appear in all these expressions:\n\n- For $(x+2)^{5},(x+2)^{3},$ and $(x+2)^{6},$ the largest power is $6.$\n\n- For $(y-7)^{7},(y-7)^{6},$ and $(y-7)^{6},$ the largest power is $7.$\n\nNow, we raise $(x+2)$ and $(y-7)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((x+2)^{5}(y-7)^{7},(x+2)^{3}(y-7)^{6},(x+2)^{6}(y-7)^{6})=(x+2)^{6}\\cdot (y-7)^{7}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{LCM}(12(x+2)^{5}(y-7)^{7},\\,8(x+2)^{3}(y-7)^{6},\\,9(x+2)^{6}(y-7)^{6}) & = \\\\ 72\\cdot (x+2)^{6}\\cdot (y-7)^{7} & = \\\\ 72(x+2)^{6}(y-7)^{7} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518661 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518654 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518654 1071 "The least common multiple of $6(y-5)^{4},$ $\\,4(y-5)^{3},$ and $8(y-5)^{2}$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518654 1072 #uuid "6d275065-3a51-5977-88eb-cbf1d26df201" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518654 1074 "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of three polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(6,4,8):$\n\n- Multiples of $6:$ $\\,6,\\,12,\\,18,24,\\,30,…$\n\n- Multiples of $4:$ $\\,4,\\,8,\\,12,\\,16,\\,20,\\,24,\\,28,…$\n\n- Multiples of $8:$ $\\,8,\\,16,\\,24,\\,32,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(6,4,8)=24.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(y-5)^{4},$ $(y-5)^{3},$ and $(y-5)^{2},$ we raise $(y-5)$ to the largest power that appears in all these expressions, which is $4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((y-5)^{4},\\,(y-5)^{3},\\,(y-5)^{2})=(y-5)^{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(6(y-5)^{4},\\,4(y-5)^{3},\\,8(y-5)^{2})=24\\cdot (y-5)^{4}=24(y-5)^{4}\n$$" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518655 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518665 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518655 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518655 1071 "The least common multiple of the polynomials $20(x-8)^{5}(y-9)(x-3)^{3},\\,20(x-8)(y-9)^{4}(x-3),$ and $8(x-8)^{2}(y-9)(x-3)^{4}$ is {{field-1}}." 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518655 1072 #uuid "7077f7cc-4a75-5a00-8932-98780a67e222" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518655 1074 "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of three polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(20,20,8):$\n\n- Multiples of $20:$ $\\,20,\\,40,\\,60,…$\n\n- Multiples of $20:$ $\\,20,\\,40,\\,60,…$\n\n- Multiples of $8:$ $\\,8,\\,16,\\,24,\\,32,\\,40,\\,48,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(20,20,8)=40.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(x-8)^{5}(y-9)(x-3)^{3},(x-8)(y-9)^{4}(x-3),(x-8)^{2}(y-9)(x-3)^{4},$ we find the largest powers for $(x-8)$, $(y-9)$, $(x-3)$ that appear in all these expressions:\n\n- For $(x-8)^{5},$ $(x-8),$ and $(x-8)^{2},$ the largest power is $5.$\n\n- For $(y-9),$ $(y-9)^{4},$ and $(y-9),$ the largest power is $4.$\n\n- For $(x-3)^{3},$ $(x-3),$ and $(x-3)^{4},$ the largest power is $4.$\n\nNow, we raise $(x-8)$, $(y-9)$, $(x-3)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((x-8)^{5}(y-9)(x-3)^{3},\\,(x-8)(y-9)^{4}(x-3),\\,(x-8)^{2}(y-9)(x-3)^{4})=(x-8)^{5}\\cdot (y-9)^{4}\\cdot (x-3)^{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{LCM}(20(x-8)^{5}(y-9)(x-3)^{3},\\,20(x-8)(y-9)^{4}(x-3),\\,8(x-8)^{2}(y-9)(x-3)^{4}) & = \\\\ 40\\cdot (x-8)^{5}\\cdot (y-9)^{4}\\cdot (x-3)^{4} & = \\\\ 40(x-8)^{5}(y-9)^{4}(x-3)^{4} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518656 1080 "12(x+5)^{4}(y+2)^{4}(x+4)^{5}" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518656 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518656 1083 #uuid "801a9977-f2c2-5b4a-9457-87492856bb2b" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518657 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518643 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518657 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518657 1071 "The least common multiple of the polynomials $15(x+2)^{5}(y+5)^{3}(x-6)^{2},\\,3(x+2)(y+5)^{5}(x-6)^{3},$ and $2(x+2)^{2}(y+5)(x-6)^{5}$ is {{field-1}}." 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518657 1072 #uuid "8b0ee8ef-af5e-530e-9db3-c1009c569dc0" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518657 1074 "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of three polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(15,3,2):$\n\n- Multiples of $15:$ $\\,15,\\,30,\\,45,…$\n\n- Multiples of $3:$ $\\,3,\\,6,\\,…,\\,27,\\,30,\\,33,…$\n\n- Multiples of $2:$ $\\,2,\\,4,\\,…,\\,28,\\,30,\\,32,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(15,3,2)=30.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(x+2)^{5}(y+5)^{3}(x-6)^{2},(x+2)(y+5)^{5}(x-6)^{3},(x+2)^{2}(y+5)(x-6)^{5},$ we find the largest powers for $(x+2)$, $(y+5)$, $(x-6)$ that appear in all these expressions:\n\n- For $(x+2)^{5},$ $(x+2),$ and $(x+2)^{2},$ the largest power is $5.$\n\n- For $(y+5)^{3},$ $(y+5)^{5},$ and $(y+5),$ the largest power is $5.$\n\n- For $(x-6)^{2},$ $(x-6)^{3},$ and $(x-6)^{5},$ the largest power is $5.$\n\nNow, we raise $(x+2)$, $(y+5)$, $(x-6)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((x+2)^{5}(y+5)^{3}(x-6)^{2},\\,(x+2)(y+5)^{5}(x-6)^{3},\\,(x+2)^{2}(y+5)(x-6)^{5})=(x+2)^{5}\\cdot (y+5)^{5}\\cdot (x-6)^{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{LCM}(15(x+2)^{5}(y+5)^{3}(x-6)^{2},\\,3(x+2)(y+5)^{5}(x-6)^{3},\\,2(x+2)^{2}(y+5)(x-6)^{5}) & = \\\\ 30\\cdot (x+2)^{5}\\cdot (y+5)^{5}\\cdot (x-6)^{5} & = \\\\ 30(x+2)^{5}(y+5)^{5}(x-6)^{5} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518658 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518659 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518658 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518658 1071 "The least common multiple of $10(y-3)^{4},$ $\\,8(y-3)^{2},$ and $5(y-3)^{3}$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139535575 17592186045443 true] [17592186518658 1072 #uuid "8e9e3ba8-01a0-5ec4-9b2c-bbbee